  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match rheumatoid arthritis PRS candidates are present (e.g., PGS001310, PGS001875, PGS002088, PGS002745, PGS004817/4819, and the 2023 comparative-family scores PGS003994–PGS004163). Among these, PGS004163 is best supported by a combination of strong phenotype alignment (trait_efo=rheumatoid arthritis; phenotyping_reported=Seropositive RA, still within RA), method structure (MultiPRS.CV suggests multi-score aggregation/ensemble, which domain knowledge flags as the strongest method property), and the strongest reported full-model discrimination within a coherent same-publication RA cluster (AUROC≈0.747 and R²≈0.138) with no added covariates listed (covariates=0), making the metric less likely to be driven by heavy clinical covariate leakage compared with candidates that include extensive lifestyle/environment covariates (e.g., PGS004326) or are pan-trait framework scores (e.g., PGS001310). Limitation: the key reported metrics here are full-model (not explicitly PGS-only), and the phenotype is seropositive RA rather than all RA, so while it is a strong direct match, uncertainty remains about standalone PRS-only performance and generalization to broader RA definitions."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates for the target concept (uterine cancer) are present, including exact/near-exact uterine endpoints (PGS000541, PGS000542, PGS001795, PGS003740) and the clinically dominant subtype endometrial/uterine endometrial carcinoma (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002300, PGS002735-PGS002737, PGS003381, PGS004244). Per domain guidance, endometrial carcinoma is a strong direct match for uterine cancer/uterine carcinoma and endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported) should drive ranking.

PGS003381 is preferred because it has high endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: uterine endometrial carcinoma), uses a genome-wide method with large variant count (lassosum; 529,365 variants), and reports supportive discrimination evidence (full-model AUROC 0.761; R² 0.11) with basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs). This combination is structurally stronger than the exact-label uterine cancer candidates from PRSweb (PGS000541/PGS000542), which are sparse (18–20 variants) with modest AUROC (~0.57), and stronger than other small-N uterine corpus candidates (e.g., PGS003740; n=372, limited metrics). 

Limitation: available performance is largely full-model (not explicit PGS-only AUROC/R²) and covariate sets differ across studies; additionally, there is meaningful competition from another genome-wide, direct-label uterine candidate (PGS001795; PRS-CS-auto; 911,692 variants; AUROC 0.66; reported PRS-comparable R² 0.001948), making the best choice not unequivocal from metadata alone."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct-match or near-direct-match to the target concept of nodular goiter: two UKB portability-framework scores for nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) and several disease-focused multi-biobank thyroid-disease scores for benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263) plus two case-control contrast models (thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter; PGS005273–PGS005274). For a generic nodular goiter target, the benign nodular goiter models are the closest endpoint match; the carcinoma-vs-benign contrasts are less endpoint-faithful because they are differential-diagnosis classifiers rather than disease-risk PRSs.

Within the benign nodular goiter family, PGS005262 is best supported by the visible evidence: it uses a genome-wide continuous shrinkage method (PRSCS) with a very large variant count (1,085,173) and shows the highest reported discrimination among its close siblings (full-model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5876 for PGS005263 and 0.5854 for the P+T model PGS005261). Compared with the UKB portability models (PGS001814/PGS002022), it also has a more disease-focused multi-cohort development context.

Main limitation: the available performance evidence for PGS005262 is primarily a full-model AUROC with covariates listed as Unknown (no explicit PRS-only AUROC/R2), so comparability and the true standalone PRS contribution are uncertain; therefore the choice is direct-match but not high-confidence/high-quality based on the provided metadata alone."}
